<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ingolstadt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ingolstadt is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. From 1392 to 1447 it was the capital of the Duchy of Bavaria-Ingolstadt, and in 1472 Duke Louis IX of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>'s first university there, which moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in the early 19th century. It was long a Bavarian fortress and is now the headquarters of the carmaker Audi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ingolstadt.jpg" alt="Ingolstadt" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: wuppertaler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GER_Ingolstadt_Rathausplatz_013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ingolstadt is an independent city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>. The old town stands about 374 metres above sea level in a wide, flat basin, where the river Schutter reaches the Danube from the west. The Ingolstadt basin borders the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-jura">Franconian Jura</a>, and lowland forests of the Danube reach into the urban area. The river there is between 80 and 100 metres wide and flows from west to east past the old town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The place was first mentioned in a document of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> of 6 February 806 as Ingoldes stat, the place of a person named Ingold. It was granted city status around 1250. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the capitals of the Bavarian duchies, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a>. Louis VII, Duke of Bavaria ordered the building of the New Castle and of the Church of Our Lady, a Gothic hall church begun in 1425 as the intended burial place of the dukes of Bavaria-Ingolstadt.</p><p>The university founded in 1472 became an influential defender of the Catholic Church in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> era, led by scholars such as Johann Eck. In 1516 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-duke-of-bavaria">William IV, Duke of Bavaria</a> wrote and signed the Bavarian Reinheitsgebot in the city. On 30 April 1632 the German field marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly">Johann Tserclaes, Count of Tilly</a> died there during a Swedish siege. Ingolstadt was the first fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to hold out for the whole length of a Swedish siege, and the Swedes withdrew. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> inside the fortress shot the horse from under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a>, and its remains are kept in the city museum.</p><p>Adam Weishaupt, who founded the Order of Illuminati in the city in 1776, was born there in 1748. After the French invasion of 1799 the fortress was demolished and the university was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>. The Bavarian fortress, in use from 1537 to 1930, held the future French president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> as a prisoner of war during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. In 1945 the car maker Auto Union arrived, and it was formally re-founded in Ingolstadt in 1949, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a> aid. It became the modern Audi company after Volkswagen took it over in 1964.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The historic old town has been preserved, with Gothic buildings such as the old ducal castle of about 1255, the Kreuztor of 1385 and the Old City Hall. The New Castle and the Tilly redoubt house the Bavarian Army Museum, the oldest museum in the city. The Old Anatomy building of 1723 to 1736 holds the German Museum of Medical History, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-shelley">Mary Shelley</a> set part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a> in the city. The city has two universities and Audi is the largest employer in the region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ingolstadt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the later Middle Ages towns such as Munich, founded by Henry the Lion in 1158, Landshut and <b>Ingolstadt</b> grew under ducal protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Bavarian partition of 1392 Stephen III received the lands around <b>Ingolstadt</b>, scattered across Upper Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-ingolstadt">Duchy of Bavaria-Ingolstadt</a></li><li><blockquote>Landshut, seat of the Lower Bavarian dukes since the 13th century, became capital of the wealthiest share, which gained part of the Duchy of Bavaria-Straubing in 1429 and all of <b>Ingolstadt</b> in 1447.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a></li></ul></section>
